WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.505 --> 00:00:02.880
SPEAKER 1: The process of
writing and running SQL queries

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.220
doesn't always follow
a straight line.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.740
A particular query can
be in constant iteration

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.260
while you use it to explore
and clean up your data,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.440
or as you fine tune it to
optimize its performance.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.650
In today's episode, we highlight
the ways to save and share

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.200
queries in BigQuery
to help you navigate

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.633
these bends in the road.

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.577
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.140
So you ran a query last
Friday for your colleague

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.640
and sent them the results.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.380
All done, right?

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.010
Wrong.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.510
It's Monday morning,
and now there's

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:39.230
another request for additional
data fields on the same query.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.610
The new request requires you to
join data from another table--

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.990
a join which you actually
did in a separate query

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.460
a few weeks ago.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.280
But you can't quite
remember how you did it.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.640
Do you really want to write
a new SQL query from scratch?

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.260
No.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.550
I mean, it's great practice,
but it's not always

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:56.990
the most practical.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.090
Luckily, BigQuery
has features built

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.280
in that allow you to
view your query history,

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.470
proactively save
specific queries,

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.430
and share queries with others.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.420
Let's take a look at each of
these features a bit further.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.910
Your first line of defense
for revisiting past queries

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.370
in BigQuery is
your query history.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.710
This feature is automatic
and includes the text

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.630
of all queries you have run.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.640
You can view your query
history in the console

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.880
by selecting Query History
in the top left-hand nav.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.440
Click on a specific
query and you'll

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.720
see the text of the query and
be able to open it in the editor

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.780
so you can modify and rerun it.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.840
Just note, this history is
limited to 1,000 queries

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.910
and looks back up to six months.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.090
Going a step further, you can
decide to proactively name

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.250
and save a specific query.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.370
Just enter the query, valid
or not, in the editor.

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.500
Then click Save
Query, located just

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.460
to the right of the Run button.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.450
Give the query a name
and then click Save.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.190
You can find a list
of your saved queries

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.230
in the left-hand nav.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.840
Similar to the functionality
in your query history,

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.360
you can now click to open
the query in the editor,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.400
modify it, and run it.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.860
If you want to share one of
your queries with a colleague,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.110
you can create shared queries.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.900
You can create a shared query
by first saving a query,

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.620
turning on link sharing, and
then sharing the unique URL.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.930
A shared query provides
the query text only,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.780
so users that open
your shared query

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.700
will still need to
have appropriate access

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.520
to the tables referenced in
the query to be able to run it.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.710
For more information on
how to share access to data

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.330
sets using Identity and Access
Management, or IAM, watch

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:32.300
our last episode, "Managing
Access with Cloud IAM,"

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.010
linked in the description below.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.050
Other users will be
restricted from editing

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.030
your personal saved queries.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.070
If you're looking to make
saved queries editable

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.630
by your team
members, you'll need

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.510
to change the saved query
visibility to Project

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.000
and then grant your team
members the permissions to get,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.760
list, and update saved
queries on your project.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.150
So now that you have the
text of the query covered,

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.940
what about the query results?

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.100
Just as BigQuery automatically
saves your query history,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.370
it also by default
caches the results

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.970
of your successfully run
queries in temporary tables.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.460
These tables expire
after 24 hours.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.980
And within this time, you
can browse the results

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.260
through your query history.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.360
Just select the query
and scroll to find

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.400
a link to the temporary table.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.940
Here you can preview the data
and also export the full table

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:21.860
to cloud storage.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:25.160
If you rerun the query, BigQuery
will use the cached results

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.910
if it can, meaning if you are
still within the 24-hour period

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.910
and the underlying data
has not had any changes.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:36.440
By using cached results, you
won't incur any query costs.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.170
Temporary tables are available
only to the specific user

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:40.830
that runs the query.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.200
So if you're looking to
share the query results,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.460
you should consider saving
them to a permanent table.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.860
You can make the switch from
saving in a temporary table

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.590
to a permanent table by
opening the query settings

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.720
and setting a destination
table for the results.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:58.210
Click Run.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:03:59.950
And after the query
is complete, you

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.650
will see the new table
in the left-hand nav.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:05.370
You can then use IAM to
share the destination data

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.470
set or table with
your collaborators.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.020
It's important to note,
permanent tables will

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:14.620
incur storage costs, while
temporary tables do not.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.180
Another method for
sharing results

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.430
is to use the option to
save them immediately

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:19.750
after running your query.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.579
Click the Save Results
button and choose

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:24.100
between several options
for saving results--

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.580
locally, in Google Drive, or
directly to a Google Sheet.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.640
We've now reviewed how to save
and share query text and query

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.610
results.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.860
But there is one more tool to
cover, which is called views.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.870
A view is a virtual
table defined by a query.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.050
A view can be queried just
like you query a table.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:43.180
Views are listed next
to tables in a data set,

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.580
denoted with a different icon.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.860
When running a query
that references a view,

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:52.220
BigQuery will also run the
query that defines that view.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.460
So why would views be helpful?

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.890
Views can help hide
the complexity of data

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.870
by allowing you to
join multiple tables

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.210
into a single virtual
table ready to query.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.930
Views can be a good alternative
to sharing query text,

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.180
because then you can take
advantage of the finer grain

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.520
controls available
in Cloud IAM to share

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.530
the view and the
underlying tables.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:13.828
Views do not require storage.

00:05:13.828 --> 00:05:15.370
Since they're virtual
tables, there's

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.790
not a storage cost
associated with them.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.600
To create a view,
write the query

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.700
that defines the view
in the Query Editor

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:23.488
and then click Save View.

00:05:23.488 --> 00:05:25.030
Choose the project
and data set where

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:28.060
you'd like the view to live,
and then give the view a name.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.590
Click Save.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:31.930
The view will populate under
the destination data set

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:36.310
and can be queried just
like you query a table.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.310
Now that you know how to
save and share queries,

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.020
it's time for you to try.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.180
If you haven't created
your BigQuery sandbox,

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.340
do so by following
the link below.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.640
Once you're in the console,
click Add Data, Explore Public

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.460
Data Sets, and then pick
out your favorite one.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.220
Write a query and share it with
a friend and in the comments

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.450
below, if you like.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.400
If you want to learn
more about BigQuery,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.957
check out the rest
of our playlist.

00:05:57.957 --> 00:05:59.790
And don't forget to
subscribe to our channel

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:01.800
on YouTube for
other great content.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.050
Look out for our next episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.150
And remember, stay curious.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:10.400
[THEME MUSIC]

